</table>

<h2>4. The Doji Guard — Filtering Ambiguous Bars</h2>
<p>A <strong>doji</strong> is a bar where the body is so small that neither buyers nor sellers won — the result is ambiguous. Engulfing signals from doji bars are meaningless because you can't say the previous bar was "clearly red" or "clearly green".</p>

<pre><code><span class="cm"># strategies/engulfing.py</span>
<span class="cls">DOJI_THRESHOLD</span> = <span class="num">0.0005</span>  <span class="cm"># 0.05% of price — body smaller than this → doji</span>

<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_is_doji</span>(bar: pd.Series) -> bool:
    <span class="kw">return</span> abs(bar[<span class="str">"Close"</span>] - bar[<span class="str">"Open"</span>]) < <span class="cls">DOJI_THRESHOLD</span> * bar[<span class="str">"Close"</span>]</code></pre>

<p>If either <code>prev</code> or <code>curr</code> is a doji, <code>generate_signal</code> returns <span class="tag hold">HOLD</span> immediately — before checking any engulfing conditions. This prevents false signals on low-volatility bars.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Why 0.05%?</strong> At $100/share, this means a body smaller than $0.05. A 5-minute bar with a $0.05 body means essentially no net movement — price opened and closed at nearly the same level, so calling it "bullish" or "bearish" is meaningless.
</div>

<h2>5. The Full Detector — Reading the Real Code</h2>
<p>This is the actual <code>strategies/engulfing.py</code> from this project, annotated:</p>

<pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> strategies.base <span class="kw">import</span> BaseStrategy, Signal
<span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd

<span class="cls">DOJI_THRESHOLD</span> = <span class="num">0.0005</span>

<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_is_doji</span>(bar: pd.Series) -> bool:
    <span class="cm"># body size as fraction of price — both sides of equality matter</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> abs(bar[<span class="str">"Close"</span>] - bar[<span class="str">"Open"</span>]) < <span class="cls">DOJI_THRESHOLD</span> * bar[<span class="str">"Close"</span>]

<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="cls">EngulfingStrategy</span>(BaseStrategy):
    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">generate_signal</span>(<span class="kw">self</span>, prev: pd.Series, curr: pd.Series, bar_index: <span class="kw">int</span> = <span class="num">0</span>) -> Signal:

        <span class="cm"># GUARD — bail early on doji; order matters (cheap check first)</span>
        <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">_is_doji</span>(prev) <span class="kw">or</span> <span class="fn">_is_doji</span>(curr):
            <span class="kw">return</span> Signal(<span class="str">"HOLD"</span>, curr[<span class="str">"Close"</span>], bar_index)

        <span class="cm"># ── BULLISH ENGULFING ──────────────────────────────────────────</span>
        prev_red   = prev[<span class="str">"Open"</span>] > prev[<span class="str">"Close"</span>]   <span class="cm"># prev bar is bearish</span>
        curr_green = curr[<span class="str">"Close"</span>] > curr[<span class="str">"Open"</span>]  <span class="cm"># curr bar is bullish</span>

        <span class="kw">if</span> prev_red <span class="kw">and</span> curr_green:
            <span class="cm"># curr body must fully cover prev body</span>
            <span class="kw">if</span> curr[<span class="str">"Open"</span>] <= prev[<span class="str">"Close"</span>] <span class="kw">and</span> curr[<span class="str">"Close"</span>] >= prev[<span class="str">"Open"</span>]:
                <span class="kw">return</span> Signal(<span class="str">"BUY"</span>, curr[<span class="str">"Close"</span>], bar_index)

        <span class="cm"># ── BEARISH ENGULFING ──────────────────────────────────────────</span>
        prev_green = prev[<span class="str">"Open"</span>] < prev[<span class="str">"Close"</span>]   <span class="cm"># prev bar is bullish</span>
        curr_red   = curr[<span class="str">"Close"</span>] < curr[<span class="str">"Open"</span>]  <span class="cm"># curr bar is bearish</span>

        <span class="kw">if</span> prev_green <span class="kw">and</span> curr_red:
            <span class="kw">if</span> curr[<span class="str">"Open"</span>] >= prev[<span class="str">"Close"</span>] <span class="kw">and</span> curr[<span class="str">"Close"</span>] <= prev[<span class="str">"Open"</span>]:
                <span class="kw">return</span> Signal(<span class="str">"SELL"</span>, curr[<span class="str">"Close"</span>], bar_index)

        <span class="cm"># Everything else — no pattern</span>
        <span class="kw">return</span> Signal(<span class="str">"HOLD"</span>, curr[<span class="str">"Close"</span>], bar_index)</code></pre>

<div class="callout good">
  <strong>Key design choice:</strong> <code>generate_signal</code> receives exactly two <code>pd.Series</code> rows — the previous bar and the current bar. It has no access to the rest of the DataFrame. This structural constraint makes lookahead physically impossible.
</div>

<h2>6. Lookahead Bias — The Most Dangerous Backtest Bug</h2>

<p><strong>Lookahead bias</strong> means your signal calculation uses information that would not have been available at the moment the signal was generated. It makes backtests look better than they will perform in live trading.</p>

<div class="callout danger">
  <strong>Classic lookahead mistake:</strong><br>
  <code>df["signal"] = df["engulfing"].shift(-1)</code><br>
  Using <code>shift(-1)</code> peeks one bar into the future — the signal at bar N uses bar N+1's prices.
</div>

<p>In the engulfing detector, lookahead is prevented by design:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Mistake (introduces bias)</th><th>Correct (no bias)</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>curr["Close"] = df.iloc[i+1]["Close"]</code></td>
    <td>Pass only <code>df.iloc[i-1]</code> and <code>df.iloc[i]</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Using <code>df.rolling().apply()</code> over a window that includes future bars</td>
    <td>Pure two-bar function — no DataFrame slice</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Signal at bar N opens trade at bar N's Close (also lookahead — you can't trade on a price until the bar is closed)</td>
    <td><code>PaperEngine</code> opens at bar <strong>N+1's Open</strong> — the first price available after the signal is confirmed</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<p>The bar-by-bar replay in <code>strategies/paper_engine.py</code> enforces this at the execution level: even if the signal is generated at bar N, the trade entry is always <code>df.iloc[i+1]["Open"]</code>.</p>

<h2>7. Often Confused With: Engulfing vs Inside Bar</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th></th><th>Engulfing</th><th>Inside Bar (Harami)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Which bar is bigger?</td><td>Current (second) bar is larger</td><td>Previous (first) bar is larger</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Relationship</td><td>Curr body ⊇ prev body</td><td>Curr body ⊆ prev body</td></tr>
