  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p>The bearish engulfing condition requires curr.Open >= prev.Close. Why >= (greater-than-or-equal) rather than strictly greater than?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The boundary case (opening exactly at the previous close) is a valid engulfing setup — there's no gap but the current bar still engulfs the previous body.">To allow for rounding errors in float comparison.</li>
      <li data-correct="true" data-why="If curr.Open == prev.Close exactly (no gap up), the curr bar still completely engulfs the prev body as long as curr.Close ≤ prev.Open. The boundary case is a legitimate signal.">Because a gap of exactly zero (curr opens precisely at prev close) still forms a valid engulfing pattern.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="yfinance prices are floats, but the >= choice is about trading logic (boundary validity), not floating-point precision.">Because yfinance returns floats which are never exactly equal.</li>
